sweet-jetsam-limit-print (audit 1 item 7 (private notes)): READ-ONLY diagnostic. Prints the per-process
footprint limit and the current phys footprint (MB) of the process the ledger callback is about to kill with
JETSAM_REASON_MEMORY_PERPROCESSLIMIT (reason ns 1 code 0x7, e.g. driverkitd), so "reason ns 1 code 0x7: (none)" gains numbers.
Changes no limit and no kill decision; only a printf in the fatal branch of memorystatus_on_ledger_footprint_exceeded, current_proc()
being the offender. Facts, read from source: the function is the only creator of that reason (bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c:4252
pristine), it is not under CONFIG_JETSAM (it is compiled on this config, line 4225); get_task_phys_footprint_limit() is bytes
(bsd/kern/kern_exit.c:452 shifts it >> 20 for the MB field of EXC_RESOURCE). Not run on hardware.
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c
@@ -4249,6 +4249,16 @@
 		 * If this process has no high watermark or has a fatal task limit, then we have been invoked because the task
 		 * has violated either the system-wide per-task memory limit OR its own task limit.
 		 */
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+		{
+			extern uint64_t get_task_phys_footprint(task_t);
+			extern uint64_t get_task_phys_footprint_limit(task_t);
+			printf("SWEET JETSAM: %s[%d] per-process limit exceeded: limit %llu MB footprint %llu MB\n",
+			    p->p_name, proc_getpid(p),
+			    get_task_phys_footprint_limit(proc_task(p)) >> 20,
+			    get_task_phys_footprint(proc_task(p)) >> 20);
+		}
+#endif
 		jetsam_reason = os_reason_create(OS_REASON_JETSAM, JETSAM_REASON_MEMORY_PERPROCESSLIMIT);
 		if (jetsam_reason == NULL) {
 			memorystatus_log_error("task_exceeded footprint: failed to allocate jetsam reason\n");
